course: history
lesson: 9
title: Ancient China: dynasties, the First Emperor and the Great Wall
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Chinese civilisation grew along two great rivers, the Yellow River and the Yangtze. The Yellow River's floods brought rich soil but also terrible disasters.
explain: China was ruled by dynasties, families passing power from parent to child, like a football club owned by one family for generations. People believed Heaven gave a good ruler the right to rule, and took it away when floods, famine and rebellion came.
explain: In 221 BC the king of Qin conquered all the warring kingdoms and called himself the First Emperor. He was buried with about 8,000 life-size clay soldiers, each with a different face.
explain: The Great Wall is not one wall but many, built over 2,000 years against horse-riding raiders from the north. And no, you cannot see it from the Moon: it is very long but far too narrow.
fact: Chinese civilisation grew up along the Yellow River and the Yangtze, and the Yellow River's floods brought rich soil but also terrible disasters.
fact: China was ruled by dynasties, families whose rule passed from parent to child, such as the Shang, Zhou, Qin, Han, Tang, Song and Ming.
fact: Shang kings, over 3,000 years ago, asked their ancestors questions by heating bones and shells until they cracked, then wrote on them; these oracle bones hold the oldest Chinese writing known.
fact: The Chinese believed Heaven gave a good ruler the right to rule, the Mandate of Heaven, and that floods, famine and rebellion showed a ruler had lost it.
fact: In 221 BC the king of Qin conquered all the warring kingdoms and called himself Qin Shi Huang, the First Emperor; the English name China may come from Qin.
fact: The First Emperor was buried with about 8,000 life-size clay soldiers, horses and chariots, the Terracotta Army, each soldier with a different face, probably to guard him after death.
fact: Farmers digging a well found the Terracotta Army in 1974.
fact: The Great Wall is many walls built over 2,000 years to keep out horse-riding raiders from the north, and most of the stone wall visited today was built by the Ming dynasty about 500 years ago.
fact: You cannot see the Great Wall from the Moon with your own eyes; it is long but too narrow.
fact: Soldiers, farmers and prisoners built the walls with earth, stone and brick; the work was very hard and many builders died.
fact: The Han dynasty ruled for about 400 years, and most Chinese people still call themselves the Han people.
fact: For centuries Chinese officials were chosen by hard written exams on classic books, not only by birth.
exercise: Along which two rivers did Chinese civilisation grow? | The Yellow River and the Yangtze | The Nile and the Indus, far to the west | The Tigris and the Ganges | The Thames and the Rhine
exercise: What did the Yellow River's floods bring? | Rich soil but also disasters | Only gold and silver washed down from the hills | Snow and ice in the middle of every summer | Nothing at all, the river never flooded
exercise: What is a dynasty? | A family whose rule passes from parent to child | A group of voters choosing a new leader each year | A kind of flat-bottomed Chinese boat | A wall built across the mountains
exercise: Which of these was a Chinese dynasty? | The Han | The Maurya | The Gupta | The Pharaoh
exercise: What did Shang kings do with oracle bones? | Heated them till they cracked to ask their ancestors questions | Ground them into powder to make medicine for the sick | Used them as money at markets | Carved them into tiny statues of gods to put in temples
exercise: What was the Mandate of Heaven? | The belief that Heaven gave a good ruler the right to rule | A law that everyone must pray to the emperor daily | A map of the stars used by sailors | The name of the emperor's palace in the capital city
exercise: What could show a ruler had lost the Mandate of Heaven? | Floods, famine and rebellion | A long, sunny summer with no rain | A big, rich harvest of rice and millet | A new baby prince born in the palace
exercise: In which year did the king of Qin become the First Emperor? | 221 BC | 1066 AD | 776 BC | 1492 AD
exercise: Which English word may come from the name Qin? | China | Chopsticks | Tea | Kung fu
exercise: About how many clay soldiers are in the Terracotta Army? | About 8,000 | About 80 | About 800,000 | About 8 million
exercise: What is special about the faces of the clay soldiers? | Each one is different | They all have the same face | They have no faces | They are made of gold
exercise: Who found the Terracotta Army, and when? | Farmers digging a well in 1974 | Soldiers building a road in 1800 | Tourists on holiday in 2010 | Marco Polo in 1275
exercise: Why was the Great Wall built? | To keep out horse-riding raiders from the north | To stop the sea from flooding the farms of China | To show travellers the way to India | To keep pandas and tigers from leaving China
exercise: Who built most of the stone wall visited today? | The Ming dynasty, about 500 years ago | The First Emperor alone, in one single year | The Romans, who sent builders to help | The Han, in one long summer
exercise: Who did the hard work of building the walls? | Soldiers, farmers and prisoners | Robots and machines brought from far away | Only the emperor's own family and servants | Volunteers who came from Europe
exercise: How were many Chinese officials chosen for centuries? | By hard written exams | By a running race | Only by being born rich | By throwing dice
test: my teacher said u can see the great wall from the moon. true? | No, it is far too narrow to see with your eyes | Yes, it is so long that it glows at night | Yes, but only in winter when it snows | No, because the Moon is always covered in clouds
test: A tour guide says the whole Great Wall was built by one emperor. What is closer to the truth? | Many walls were built over 2,000 years by many dynasties | One emperor built the whole wall in a single year | The Romans built most of it to help China | It was only built in the 1900s for tourists
test: A dynasty has floods, hunger and rebels all in one year. What would people think? | The ruler may have lost the Mandate of Heaven | The ruler is doing a great job and Heaven is pleased | Heaven is very pleased with the dynasty | Nothing, because rulers in China never changed
test: If officials were chosen only by birth, what would be lost? | Clever poor people could never get the job | Exams would be much harder | Nobody would be rich | There would be no emperor
test: Why do most Chinese people call themselves the Han people? | The Han dynasty was so long and important | Han means "the people of the wall" in Chinese | The Han were the first farmers by the Yellow River | Because of a famous film about a hero called Han
test: farmers just digging a well and find 8000 clay soldiers?? what'd they find? | The Terracotta Army of the First Emperor | A Roman army camp | A Viking ship | A Maya city
test: Why would the First Emperor want an army of clay soldiers in his tomb? | To guard him in the afterlife | To play games with in the dark at night | To scare away farmers who came to dig there | To sell to traders on the Silk Road after he died
test: How is a dynasty like a family business? | Power is handed from parent to child | Everyone votes for the boss each year | It has no leader | It always ends after one year
test: Why do the oracle bones matter so much to historians? | They hold the oldest known Chinese writing | They were made of solid gold from the emperor's mines | They show a map of China | They are the bones of a real dragon from the river
test: What does it tell us that many wall-builders died? | The work was very hard and dangerous | The wall was built for fun | Nobody was ever hurt | The builders were robots
exam: Identify the ruler who, in the year 221 before the common era, unified the warring kingdoms and assumed the title of First Emperor. | Qin Shi Huang | Confucius | Kublai Khan | Zheng He
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the visibility of the Great Wall from the Moon. | It is not visible to the unaided eye, being too narrow | It is plainly visible at all times | It is visible solely at night | It is visible solely in winter
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the doctrine designated the Mandate of Heaven? | Heaven conferred the right to rule upon a virtuous ruler and might withdraw it | The emperor was to be elected annually | Heaven forbade the construction of walls | Every subject was obliged to become an official
exam: Ascertain the circumstances of the discovery of the Terracotta Army. | It was uncovered by farmers digging a well in 1974 | It was described by Marco Polo in 1275 on his travels | It was excavated by Roman soldiers | It was found by sailors in the year 1500
